an3.68:1.4 | S: Greed, hate, and delusion. | T: Passion, aversion, & delusion.
an3.68:1.5 | S: These are the three things. | T: These are the three qualities.
an3.68:1.6 | S: What’s the difference between them?’ | T: Now what is the difference, what the distinction, what the distinguishing factor among these three qualities?’—
an3.68:1.7 | S: How would you answer them?” | T: when thus asked, how would you answer those wanderers of other sects?”
an3.68:1.8 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “For us, lord, the teachings have the Blessed One as their root, their guide, & their arbitrator. It would be good if the Blessed One himself would explicate the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from the Blessed One, the monks will remember it.”
an3.68:1.9 | S: “Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, monks, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an3.68:1.10 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an3.68:1.11 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an3.68:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: | T: “Monks, if you are asked by wanderers of other sects,
an3.68:2.2 | S: ‘There are these three things. | T: ‘Friends, there are these three qualities.
an3.68:2.3 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.68:2.4 | S: Greed, hate, and delusion. | T: Passion, aversion, & delusion.
an3.68:2.5 | S: These are the three things. | T: These are the three qualities.
an3.68:2.6 | S: What’s the difference between them?’ | T: Now what is the difference, what the distinction, what the distinguishing factor among these three qualities?’—
an3.68:2.7 | S: You should answer them: | T: when thus asked, you should answer those wanderers of other sects in this way,
