sn22.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Bhaggas at Crocodile’s Bellow, in the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Bhaggas at Crocodile Haunt in the Bhesakaḷā Forest at the Deer Park.
sn22.1:1.3 | S: Then the householder Nakula’s father went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the householder Nakulapitar [Nakula’s father] went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn22.1:2.1 | S: “Sir, I’m an old man, elderly and senior. I’m advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life. My body is ailing and I’m constantly unwell. | T: “Lord, I am a feeble old man, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life. I am afflicted in body & ailing with every moment.
sn22.1:2.2 | S: I hardly ever get to see the esteemed mendicants. | T: And it’s only rarely that I get to see the Blessed One & the monks who nourish the heart.
sn22.1:2.3 | S: May the Buddha please advise me | T: May the Blessed One teach me,
sn22.1:2.4 | S: and instruct me. | T: may the Blessed One instruct me,
sn22.1:2.5 | S: It will be for my lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: for my long-term benefit & happiness.”
sn22.1:3.1 | S: “That’s so true, householder! That’s so true, householder! | T: “So it is, householder. So it is.
sn22.1:3.2 | S: For this body is ailing, swaddled in its shell. | T: The body is afflicted, weak, & encumbered.
sn22.1:3.3 | S: If anyone dragging around this body claimed to be healthy even for an hour, what is that but foolishness? | T: For who, looking after this body, would claim even a moment of true health, except through sheer foolishness?
sn22.1:3.4 | S: So you should train like this: | T: So you should train yourself:
sn22.1:3.5 | S: ‘Though my body is ailing, my mind will be healthy.’ | T: ‘Even though I may be afflicted in body, my mind will be unafflicted.’
sn22.1:3.6 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That is how you should train yourself.”
sn22.1:4.1 | S: And then the householder Nakula’s father approved and agreed with what the Buddha said. He got up from his seat, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right. Then he went up to Venerable Sāriputta, bowed, and sat down to one side. Sāriputta said to him: | T: Then the householder Nakulapitar, delighting in & approving of the Blessed One’s words, rose from his seat and—bowing down to the Blessed One and circumambulating him, keeping him to his right—went to Ven. Sāriputta and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, Ven. Sāriputta said to him,
sn22.1:4.2 | S: “Householder, your faculties are so very clear, | T: “Your faculties are clear & calm, householder,
sn22.1:4.3 | S: and your complexion is pure and bright. | T: your complexion pure.
sn22.1:4.4 | S: Did you get to hear a Dhamma talk in the Buddha’s presence today?” | T: Have you had the opportunity of listening to a Dhamma talk in the presence of the Blessed One today?”
sn22.1:5.1 | S: “What else, sir, could it be? | T: “How could it be otherwise, venerable sir?
sn22.1:5.2 | S: Just now the Buddha anointed me with the deathless ambrosia of a Dhamma talk.” | T: I have just now been sprinkled by the Blessed One with the deathless ambrosia of a Dhamma talk.”
sn22.1:5.3 | S: “But with what kind of ambrosial Dhamma talk did the Buddha anoint you?” | T: “And how were you sprinkled by the Blessed One with the deathless ambrosia of a Dhamma talk?”
sn22.1:5.4 | S: So Nakula’s father told Sāriputta all that had happened, and said, | T: “Just now I went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As I was sitting there I said to him,
sn22.1:5.5 | P: ‘ahamasmi, bhante, jiṇṇo vuḍḍho mahallako addhagato vayoanuppatto āturakāyo abhikkhaṇātaṅko. | T: ‘Lord, I am a feeble old man, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life. I am afflicted in body & ailing with every moment.
sn22.1:5.6 | P: Aniccadassāvī kho panāhaṁ, bhante, bhagavato manobhāvanīyānañca bhikkhūnaṁ. | T: And it is only rarely that I get to see the Blessed One & the monks who nourish the heart.
sn22.1:5.7 | P: Ovadatu maṁ, bhante, bhagavā; | T: May the Blessed One teach me,
sn22.1:5.8 | P: anusāsatu maṁ, bhante, bhagavā; | T: may the Blessed One instruct me,
sn22.1:5.9 | P: yaṁ mamassa dīgharattaṁ hitāya sukhāyā’ti. | T: for my long-term benefit & happiness.’
sn22.1:6.1 | P: Evaṁ vutte, maṁ, bhante, bhagavā etadavoca: | T: “When this was said, the Blessed One said to me,
sn22.1:6.2 | P: ‘evametaṁ, gahapati, evametaṁ, gahapati. | T: ‘So it is, householder. So it is.
sn22.1:6.3 | P: Āturo hāyaṁ, gahapati, kāyo aṇḍabhūto pariyonaddho. | T: The body is afflicted, weak, & encumbered.
sn22.1:6.4 | P: Yo hi, gahapati, imaṁ kāyaṁ pariharanto muhuttampi ārogyaṁ paṭijāneyya, kimaññatra bālyā? | T: For who, looking after this body, would claim even a moment of true health, except through sheer foolishness?
sn22.1:6.5 | P: Tasmātiha te, gahapati, evaṁ sikkhitabbaṁ— | T: So you should train yourself:
sn22.1:6.6 | P: āturakāyassa me sato cittaṁ anāturaṁ bhavissatīti. | T: “Even though I may be afflicted in body, my mind will be unafflicted.”
sn22.1:6.7 | P: Evañhi te, gahapati, sikkhitabban’ti. | T: That is how you should train yourself.’
sn22.1:6.8 | S: “That’s the ambrosial Dhamma talk with which I was anointed by the Buddha.” | T: That’s how I was sprinkled by the Blessed One with the deathless ambrosia of a Dhamma talk.”
sn22.1:7.1 | S: “But didn’t you feel the need to ask the Buddha the further question: | T: “But why didn’t it occur to you to question the Blessed One further:
sn22.1:7.2 | S: ‘Sir, how do you define someone ailing in body and ailing in mind, and someone ailing in body and healthy in mind’?” | T: ‘In what way is one afflicted in body & afflicted in mind? And in what way is one afflicted in body but unafflicted in mind?’
sn22.1:7.3 | S: “Sir, we would travel a long way to learn the meaning of this statement in the presence of Venerable Sāriputta. | T: “I would come from a long way away to hear the explication of these words in Ven. Sāriputta’s presence.
sn22.1:7.4 | S: May Venerable Sāriputta himself please clarify the meaning of this.” | T: It would be good if Ven. Sāriputta himself would enlighten me as to their meaning.”
sn22.1:8.1 | S: “Well then, householder, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then in that case, householder, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn22.1:8.2 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied Nakula’s father. | T: “As you say, venerable sir,” the householder Nakulapitar responded to him.
sn22.1:8.3 | S: Sāriputta said this: | T: Ven. Sāriputta said:
sn22.1:9.1 | S: “And how is one ailing in body and ailing in mind? | T: “Now, how is one afflicted in body & afflicted in mind?
sn22.1:9.2 | S: It’s when an unlearned ordinary person has not seen the noble ones, and is neither skilled nor trained in the qualities of a noble one. They’ve not seen true persons, and are neither skilled nor trained in the qualities of a true person. | T: “There is the case where an uninstructed, run-of-the-mill person—who has no regard for noble ones, is not well-versed or disciplined in their Dhamma; who has no regard for people of integrity, is not well-versed or disciplined in their Dhamma—
sn22.1:9.3 | S: They regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: assumes form to be the self, or the self as possessing form, or form as in the self, or the self as in form.
sn22.1:9.4 | S: They’re obsessed with the thought: ‘I am form, form is mine!’ | T: He is seized with the idea that ‘I am form’ or ‘Form is mine.’
sn22.1:9.5 | S: But that form of theirs decays and perishes, | T: As he is seized with these ideas, that form changes & alters.
sn22.1:9.6 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: From the change & alteration in his form, there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn22.1:10.1 | S: They regard feeling as self, self as having feeling, feeling in self, or self in feeling. | T: “He assumes feeling to be the self, or the self as possessing feeling, or feeling as in the self, or the self as in feeling.
sn22.1:10.2 | S: They’re obsessed with the thought: ‘I am feeling, feeling is mine!’ | T: He is seized with the idea that ‘I am feeling’ or ‘Feeling is mine.’
sn22.1:10.3 | S: But that feeling of theirs decays and perishes, | T: As he is seized with these ideas, that feeling changes & alters.
sn22.1:10.4 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: From the change & alteration in the feeling, there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn22.1:11.1 | S: They regard perception as self, self as having perception, perception in self, or self in perception. | T: “He assumes perception to be the self, or the self as possessing perception, or perception as in the self, or the self as in perception.
sn22.1:11.2 | S: They’re obsessed with the thought: ‘I am perception, perception is mine!’ | T: He is seized with the idea that ‘I am perception’ or ‘Perception is mine.’
sn22.1:11.3 | S: But that perception of theirs decays and perishes, | T: As he is seized with these ideas, that perception changes & alters.
sn22.1:11.4 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: From the change & alteration in the perception, there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn22.1:12.1 | S: They regard choices as self, self as having choices, choices in self, or self in choices. | T: “He assumes fabrications to be the self, or the self as possessing fabrications, or fabrications as in the self, or the self as in fabrications.
sn22.1:12.2 | S: They’re obsessed with the thought: ‘I am choices, choices are mine!’ | T: He is seized with the idea that ‘I am fabrications’ or ‘Fabrications are mine.’
sn22.1:12.3 | S: But those choices of theirs decay and perish, | T: As he is seized with these ideas, those fabrications change & alter.
sn22.1:12.4 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: From the change & alteration in the fabrications, there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.

