sn22.1:13.2 | S: They’re obsessed with the thought: ‘I am consciousness, consciousness is mine!’ | T: He is seized with the idea that ‘I am consciousness’ or ‘Consciousness is mine.’
sn22.1:13.3 | S: But that consciousness of theirs decays and perishes, | T: As he is seized with these ideas, that consciousness changes & alters.
sn22.1:13.4 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: From the change & alteration in the consciousness, there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn22.1:13.5 | S: That’s how one is ailing in body and ailing in mind. | T: “This, householder, is how one is afflicted in body and afflicted in mind.
sn22.1:14.1 | S: And how is one ailing in body and healthy in mind? | T: “And how is one afflicted in body but unafflicted in mind?
sn22.1:14.2 | S: It’s when a learned noble disciple has seen the noble ones, and is skilled and trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve seen true persons, and are skilled and trained in the teaching of the true persons. | T: There is the case where a well-instructed disciple of the noble ones—who has regard for noble ones, is well-versed & disciplined in their Dhamma; who has regard for people of integrity, is well-versed & disciplined in their Dhamma—
sn22.1:14.3 | S: They don’t regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: doesn’t assume form to be the self, or the self as possessing form, or form as in the self, or the self as in form.
sn22.1:14.4 | S: They’re not obsessed with the thought: ‘I am form, form is mine!’ | T: He is not seized with the idea that ‘I am form’ or ‘Form is mine.’
