## an3.47
an3.47:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the conditioned has these three characteristics. | T: “Monks, these three are fabricated characteristics of what is fabricated.
an3.47:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.47:1.3 | S: Arising is evident, vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: Arising is discernable, passing away is discernable, alteration [literally: otherness] while staying is discernable.
an3.47:1.4 | S: These are the three characteristics of the conditioned.” | T: “These are three fabricated characteristics of what is fabricated.
an3.47:1.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.47:2.0 | S: Characteristics of the Unconditioned | T: (none)
an3.47:2.1 | S: “The unconditioned has these three characteristics. | T: “Now these three are unfabricated characteristics of what is unfabricated.
an3.47:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.47:2.3 | S: No arising is evident, no vanishing is evident, and no change while persisting is evident. | T: No arising is discernable, no passing away is discernable, no alteration while staying is discernable.
an3.47:2.4 | S: These are the three characteristics of the unconditioned.” | T: “These are three unfabricated characteristics of what is unfabricated.” Alternative translation: “Monks, these three are fabricated characteristics of what is fabricated. Which three? Arising is discernable, passing away is discernable, alteration [literally: otherness] of what stays is discernable. “These are three fabricated characteristics of what is fabricated. “Now these three are unfabricated characteristics of what is unfabricated. Which three? No arising is discernable, no passing away is discernable, no alteration of what stays is discernable. “These are three unfabricated characteristics of what is unfabricated.”
an3.47:2.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.5
an3.5:1.1 | S: “A fool is known by three things. | T: “Monks, a person endowed with three qualities is to be recognized as a fool.
an3.5:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.5:1.3 | S: They ask a question irrationally. They answer a question irrationally. And when someone else answers a question rationally—with well-rounded, coherent, and relevant words and phrases—they disagree with it. | T: He formulates a question inappropriately. He answers a question inappropriately. When another answers a question appropriately—having chosen circumscribed, coherent words & phrases—he doesn’t approve of it.
an3.5:1.4 | S: These are the three things by which a fool is known. | T: A person endowed with these three qualities is to be recognized as a fool.
an3.5:2.1 | S: An astute person is known by three things. | T: “Monks, a person endowed with three qualities is to be recognized as a wise person.
an3.5:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.5:2.3 | S: They ask a question rationally. They answer a question rationally. And when someone else answers a question rationally—with well-rounded, coherent, and relevant words and phrases—they agree with it. | T: He formulates a question appropriately. He answers a question appropriately. When another answers a question appropriately—having chosen circumscribed, coherent words & phrases—he approves of it.
an3.5:2.4 | S: These are the three things by which an astute person is known. | T: A person endowed with these three qualities is to be recognized as a wise person.”
an3.5:2.5 | S: So you should train …” | T: (none)
an3.5:2.6 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti93
iti93:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti93:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three fires. | T: “Monks, there are these three fires.
iti93:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti93:2.3 | S: The fires of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: The fire of passion, the fire of aversion, the fire of delusion.
iti93:2.4 | S: These are the three fires.” | T: These are the three fires.”
iti93:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti93:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti93:3.1 | S: “The fire of greed burns a mortal, | T: The fire of passion burns in a mortal
iti93:3.2 | S: lustful, infatuated by sensual pleasures; | T: delighting in, smitten with sensual desires;
iti93:3.3 | S: while, fallen in the fire of hate, | T: the fire of aversion, in a malevolent person
iti93:3.4 | S: a person kills living creatures; | T: taking life;
iti93:4.1 | S: and, bewildered by the fire of delusion, | T: the fire of delusion, in a bewildered person
iti93:4.2 | S: they miss the teaching of the noble ones. | T: ignorant of the noble teaching.
iti93:4.3 | S: Not recognizing these three fires, | T: Not understanding these fires,
iti93:4.4 | S: people are caught up in substantial reality. | T: people –fond of self-identity– unreleased from Māra’s shackles,
iti93:5.1 | S: They fill the ranks of hell, | T: swell the ranks of hell,
iti93:5.2 | S: of birth as an animal, | T: the wombs of common animals,
iti93:5.3 | S: or of titans and ghosts, | T: demons, the realm of the hungry ghosts.
iti93:5.4 | S: not freed from Māra’s bonds. | T: (none)
iti93:6.1 | S: But as to those committed day and night | T: While those who, day & night, are devoted
iti93:6.2 | S: to the teaching of the Buddha: | T: to the message of the rightly self-awakened,
iti93:6.3 | S: they extinguish the fire of greed, | T: put out the fire of passion,
iti93:6.4 | S: always perceiving ugliness; | T: constantly perceiving the foul.
iti93:7.1 | S: while those supreme men | T: They, superlative people,
iti93:7.2 | S: extinguish the fire of hate with love; | T: put out the fire of aversion with good will,
iti93:7.3 | S: and the fire of delusion with the wisdom | T: and the fire of delusion with the discernment leading
iti93:7.4 | S: that leads to penetration. | T: to penetration.
iti93:8.1 | S: Having extinguished these fires, alert, | T: They, the masterful,
iti93:8.2 | S: tireless all day and night, | T: untiring by night & day,
iti93:8.3 | S: they become fully extinguished, | T: having put out [the fires],
iti93:8.4 | S: completely transcending suffering. | T: having, without remainder, comprehended stress, are, without remainder, totally unbound.
iti93:9.1 | S: Seers of the noble truths, knowledge masters, | T: They, the wise, with an attainer-of-wisdom’s noble vision,
iti93:9.2 | S: the astute, understanding rightly, | T: right gnosis,
iti93:9.3 | S: directly know the ending of rebirth, | T: directly knowing the ending of birth,
iti93:9.4 | S: they come not back to future lives.” | T: come to no further becoming.
iti93:10.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti93:10.2 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp5.5
snp5.5:1.1 | S: “I ask you, Blessed One; please tell me this,” | T: I ask you, Blessed One. Please tell me.
snp5.5:1.2 | S: said Venerable Mettagū, | T: (none)
snp5.5:1.3 | S: “for I think you are a knowledge master, evolved. | T: I regard you as an attainer-of-knowledge, developed in mind.
snp5.5:1.4 | S: Where do all these sufferings come from, | T: From what have the many forms of stress
snp5.5:1.5 | S: in all their countless forms in the world?” | T: arisen in the world?
snp5.5:2.1 | S: “You have rightly asked me of the origin of suffering,” | T: The Buddha: You ask me the source of stress.
snp5.5:2.2 | S: replied the Buddha, | T: (none)
snp5.5:2.3 | S: “I shall tell you as I understand it. | T: I’ll tell it to you as one who discerns.
snp5.5:2.4 | S: Attachment is the source of suffering | T: From acquisition as cause
snp5.5:2.5 | S: in all its countless forms in the world. | T: the many forms of stress come into being in the world.
snp5.5:3.1 | S: When an ignorant person builds up attachments, | T: Whoever, unknowing, makes acquisitions
snp5.5:3.2 | S: that dullard returns to suffering again and again. | T: —the dullard— comes to stress again & again.
snp5.5:3.3 | S: So let one who understands <j>not build up attachments, | T: Therefore, discerning, you shouldn’t create acquisitions
snp5.5:3.4 | S: contemplating the inception of suffering and rebirth.” | T: as you stay focused on the birth & origin of stress.
snp5.5:4.1 | S: “Whatever I asked you have explained to me. | T: Mettagū: What we asked, you’ve expounded.
snp5.5:4.2 | S: I ask you once more, please tell me this: | T: Now we ask something else. Please tell us.
snp5.5:4.3 | S: How do the attentive cross the flood | T: How do the enlightened cross over the flood of
snp5.5:4.4 | S: of rebirth, old age, sorrow, and lamenting? | T: birth & aging, lamentation & sorrow?
snp5.5:4.5 | S: Please, sage, answer me clearly, | T: Please, sage, declare this to me
snp5.5:4.6 | S: for truly you understand this matter.” | T: as this Dhamma has been known by you.
snp5.5:5.1 | S: “I shall extol a teaching to you,” | T: The Buddha: I will expound to you Dhamma
snp5.5:5.2 | S: replied the Buddha, | T: (none)
snp5.5:5.3 | S: “that is apparent in the present, not relying on legends. | T: —here-&-now, not quoted words—
snp5.5:5.4 | S: Having understood it, one who lives mindfully | T: knowing which, living mindfully,
